abstract: >-
  A benchmark of 5,102 labelled name pairs measuring cross-script name matching
  for sanctions and denied-party screening on Russian, Kazakh, Ukrainian,
  Belarusian, Uzbek, Kyrgyz and Tajik names. Each pair is tagged with the
  linguistic phenomenon it tests, which converts a score into an error taxonomy.
  Nineteen baselines are measured, spanning exact match, raw fuzzy matching,
  English phonetic algorithms, ICU transliteration, multilingual embeddings,
  purpose-built entity-resolution matchers and frontier language models. The
  principal finding is that purpose-built matchers recover 58.5% of true matches
  at a 1% false-positive rate against hard negatives, failing systematically on
  name structure rather than orthography, while a general-purpose language model
  given no transliteration tables recovers 97.8% at the same operating point —
  though it cannot satisfy the determinism and replayability an audited
  screening pipeline requires.
